# SRG-APP-000810-AU-000250  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must prevent the installation of
# organization-defined software and firmware components without verification
# that the component has been digitally signed using a certificate that is
# recognized and approved by the organization.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000810-AU-000250
group:           V-263571
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-003992
control_rev5:    CM-14

contribution:    Splunk can close the channel by which an app is pulled straight onto the
                 instance, confine the capability that permits an app to be installed to
                 administrative roles, and verify after the fact that the installed files
                 still match the release manifest.

governs:         authorize.conf - Used to create roles.
governs:         limits.conf - Limits on how much work searches and data processing may consume.
governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.

check:           server.conf [applicationsManagement] allowInternetAccess in false|0
check:           limits.conf [system_checks] enable_install_apps equals true ; authorize.conf [role_user] install_apps absent ; authorize.conf [role_user] edit_local_apps absent ; authorize.conf [role_power] install_apps absent ; authorize.conf [role_power] edit_local_apps absent
check:           limits.conf [system_checks] installed_files_integrity equals enabled
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
corrected:       authorize.conf [role_<roleName>] install_apps absent -> limits.conf [system_checks] enable_install_apps equals true ; authorize.conf [role_user] install_apps absent ; authorize.conf [role_user] edit_local_apps absent ; authorize.conf [role_power] install_apps absent ; authorize.conf [role_power] edit_local_apps absent
vendor:          authorize.conf [role_<roleName>] install_apps https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.3-configuration-file-reference/authorize.conf
vendor:          server.conf [applicationsManagement] allowInternetAccess https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Verification that a component is digitally signed with a certificate the
                 organization recognizes: Splunk Enterprise 10.4.2 exposes no setting that
                 requires or validates a package signature before an app loads, so this
                 part of the objective is carried by the package producer's signing, the
                 organization's software acceptance procedure, and the operating system
                 package manager's signature checking.
external:        Which software and firmware components are in scope, which the requirement
                 leaves to the organization.
external:        Firmware components, which are outside the application entirely and are
                 carried by the platform.

citation:        Central Log Server SRG SRG-APP-000810-AU-000250 check and fix text;
                 server.conf.spec:1734 (allowInternetAccess), authorize.conf.spec:760
                 ([capability::install_apps]), authorize.conf.spec:68 (capabilities
                 disabled by default), limits.conf.spec:4450 (installed_files_integrity =
                 enabled | log_only | disabled, default enabled)
finding:         The instance can reach the remote applications repository, a
                 non-administrative role holds the capability to install apps, or
                 installed-file integrity verification is off, so the application does not
                 confine component installation to the paths where a verification step can
                 be applied.
